<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!--
        This theme styles almost nothing — Compose M3 draws every pixel of real UI. What it does
        decide is the window: no action bar, and a parent an AppCompatActivity will accept.

        That parent is not a style preference, it is a hard requirement of the language switcher
        (ADR-0004): AppCompatActivity throws on a theme that does not descend from Theme.AppCompat.
        It is deliberately *not* a Material Components theme (Theme.MaterialComponents.*) — that
        would pull in com.google.android.material, a second widget library rendering nothing, when
        AppCompat's own theme is all the activity asks for.

        DayNight makes the window background follow the system dark-mode setting, which keeps the
        pixels Android owns — the window background, the frame drawn before Compose composes — in
        step with the colour scheme Theme.kt picks.

        The three items below are the app's edge-to-edge window, standing in for
        `enableEdgeToEdge()` — see `values/colors.xml` for why that call is not made and why the
        scrim is a colour resource rather than three items redefined per qualifier.

        `windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds` is what makes the other two mean anything: without it the
        platform owns the bar backgrounds and the colours are ignored. The bar *icons* are not set
        here — they follow the app's own palette rather than the system's, which a static attribute
        cannot express, so `AppTheme` writes them at runtime.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.App" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds">true</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/system_bar_scrim</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/system_bar_scrim</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The compact controls' window (`docs/phase-3.md` §2). Everything that makes this a dialog
        rather than a screen is here, because a floating window is not a property code sets — the
        platform reads these attributes when the window is created, before ControlsActivity runs a
        line.

        The parent is the same hard requirement as above and not a style preference:
        AppCompatActivity throws on a theme that does not descend from Theme.AppCompat, and the
        compact host has to be an AppCompatActivity for the language switcher (ADR-0004) and the
        night mode (ADR-0006). A Theme.MaterialComponents.*.Dialog parent would pull in a second
        widget library that renders nothing here.

        `backgroundDimEnabled` is false on purpose, and it is the one item with a safety argument
        behind it. The platform's dialog dim is a scrim the *system* draws over everything behind the
        window — a third dimming mechanism, on top of the shade's two, and outside every bound
        ShadeRampTest asserts. CLAUDE.md's composite rule can only see the two layers this app draws;
        it cannot see a system scrim, so there must not be one.

        `windowNoTitle` appears twice, once with the `android:` prefix and once without, and the
        phone is what found that out (R2). The prefixed one is the platform's; the bare one is
        AppCompat's own attribute, and it is the one `AppCompatDelegate` reads when it builds the
        sub-decor. A dialog theme shows a title by default, so with only the platform attribute set
        the window came up with a grey bar reading "Gloam debug" across the top of it. `phase-3.md`
        §2's block lists only the prefixed one; this is the correction.

        No edge-to-edge items: a floating window does not draw the system bars, and the activity
        behind it keeps owning them.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.App.Controls" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.Dialog">
        <item name="android:windowIsFloating">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <!-- Transparent so the rounded `Surface` inside actually reads as rounded. The parent
             dialog theme supplies an opaque, squared-off window background drawable, and it is
             painted by the window manager *behind* everything Compose draws — so without this the
             corner radius is there and simply has an opaque rectangle sitting under it. This is
             the platform half of a rounded dialog; the radius itself is Compose's. -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <!-- No shadow. The parent dialog theme carries an `android:windowElevation`, and a window's
             elevation is drawn by the window manager *outside* the window's own bounds — so it is
             not something the Compose `Surface` inside can decline, and setting `shadowElevation`
             to 0 there (its default already) does nothing about it. A shade this app draws is meant
             to be the darkest thing on screen; a system-drawn penumbra around the controls is one
             more layer of dimming that CLAUDE.md's composite bound cannot see. -->
        <item name="android:windowElevation">0dp</item>
        <item name="android:backgroundDimEnabled">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
